window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-0de0c9bfc707c2c7"]={"key":"ny_main-0de0c9bfc707c2c7","group":"ny_main","uid":"0de0c9bfc707c2c7","rank":14,"title":"lanternwood supplier award decision 2024q3","occupation":"buyers_purchasing_agents","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You’re the buyer at Lanternwood Trading Company responsible for the control-panel assembly line supplying Halberdine Agricultural, and Viktor Stavropoulos needs the supplier award record by Friday, 25 October 2024. The record will be shared with Jasmin Ricciardi at Halberdine, so it must support a firm decision without presenting unresolved engineering matters as settled facts. The choice is whether to renew the currently approved incumbent for the six-month call-off running 1 November 2024 through 30 April 2025 or award that call-off to the approved alternate. Forecast demand is 8,400 assemblies, averaging 1,400 per month with a peak month of 1,550. For April–June 2024, the incumbent delivered 96.4% on time, had a 1.1% incoming defect rate, averaged 18 calendar days’ lead time, and required two expedites costing $2,400. For July–September, its results were 84.7% on time, 2.8% defects, 27 days’ lead time, and 11 expedites costing $16,500. Its new quote is $118 per assembly, it states capacity of 1,600 per month, and its existing assembly is released for both Ontario, Canada and Victoria, Australia. The approved alternate’s comparable-line results were 94.8% on time, 1.4% defects, 21 days’ lead time, and three expedites costing $3,600 in April–June; July–September results were 93.9%, 1.6%, 22 days, and four expedites costing $4,800. Its quote is $111 per assembly, with $24,000 of transfer tooling and $9,000 of initial premium freight, and it states capacity of 1,500 per month. Those results come from a different assembly family. Sigrid Karlsson has not completed the alternate’s part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality reviews for either Ontario or Victoria; the present estimate is six to eight weeks, with no commitment that both reviews will close before 1 November. Lanternwood’s policy mixes mandatory release and authority controls with preferred total-cost, continuity, and performance considerations, and the last client update treated them as though they all carried the same force. A commitment whose reasonably expected purchasing and expedite cost exceeds $1,000,000 requires Viktor’s written approval before release. Tadeo Gasparyan owns supplier commercial actions, Sigrid owns engineering and quality release, and Ainsley Achterberg owns production planning and capacity confirmation. No scrap, rework, duty, downtime, line-stop, or jurisdictional compliance cost beyond the figures above has been validated, so none may be invented or presented as fact. Please do six things in the returned work: (1) make exactly one supplier-path recommendation and record the authorized direction for this call-off; (2) compare April–June with July–September, quantify the material changes, and explain why they matter; (3) show a defensible forward total-cost view while explicitly warning that the lowest quoted unit price is not necessarily the lowest total-cost decision; (4) draw a clear MUST-versus-SHOULD boundary among award controls and commercial considerations, separately address Ontario and Victoria, and preserve engineering’s authority over technical acceptance; (5) provide an executable path if an approval, release, capacity confirmation, or other required part of the award process cannot be completed in time rather than assuming the process will succeed; and (6) assign concrete actions, owners, dates, evidence or decision gates, and fallbacks. Produce `supplier_award_decision_memorandum.docx`, limited to two pages and written for Jasmin and Viktor, plus `supplier_award_action_register.xlsx`, limited to one worksheet and no more than 12 action rows, with fields for action, owner, due date, status, required evidence or decision gate, and fallback. Any recommendation, dates, costs, volumes, thresholds, or other figures appearing in both files must agree. Write both files into the top level of the working directory using exactly those filenames.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `supplier_award_decision_memorandum.docx` — External-facing executive supplier award decision memorandum comparing the two performance periods and recording one call-off direction. (Must be no more than two rendered pages.; Must make exactly one supplier-path recommendation.; Must be suitable for the named external customer and procurement director.; Must not invent unvalidated costs or make technical acceptance claims on engineering's behalf.)\n- `supplier_award_action_register.xlsx` — Executable register of award actions, owners, timing, gates, evidence, status, and fallback actions supporting the memorandum. (Must contain one worksheet.; Must contain no more than 12 action rows.; Must include action, owner, due date, status, required evidence or decision gate, and fallback information.; All shared recommendations, dates, costs, volumes, thresholds, and other figures must agree with the memorandum.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/supplier_award_action_register.xlsx","bytes":7868,"extension":".xlsx","url":"assets/3053915312fda129974c8ecdc64f7fdaf3dddaf74a4e575c2012014d1a9174dd.xlsx","sha256":"3053915312fda129974c8ecdc64f7fdaf3dddaf74a4e575c2012014d1a9174dd","preview":{"kind":"sheets","sheets":[{"name":"Award actions","row_count":14,"rows":[{"r":"1","cells":["LANTERNWOOD TRADING COMPANY"]},{"r":"2","cells":["Supplier award action register | Control-panel assembly | Halberdine Agricultural"]},{"r":"4","cells":["Decision direction: controlled incumbent renewal for 1 Nov 2024 to 30 Apr 2025, subject to MUST gates. Approved alternate remains a continuity contingency; not authorized for target-assembly production."]},{"r":"5","cells":["Control amounts use the July-September expedite run rate for each of the two call-off quarters. As of 25 Oct 2024."]},{"r":"6","cells":["No.","Action","Owner","Due date","Status","Required evidence / decision gate","Fallback"]},{"r":"7","cells":["1","Approve controlled incumbent call-off; reasonably expected purchasing plus projected expedites is $1,024,200, above $1,000,000.","Viktor Stavropoulos","45590","Open - written approval required","Signed written approval of $1,024,200 control amount before release.","Hold production release. Viktor documents whether a time-bounded validated continuity bridge is authorized."]},{"r":"8","cells":["2","Confirm incumbent allocation for 8,400 assemblies and the 1,550-unit peak month.","Ainsley Achterberg","45593","Open - capacity confirmation","Written allocation against stated 1,600/month capacity, showing 50-unit/month peak headroom and supply-plan coverage.","Hold release; Ainsley escalates the gap. Use only an approved, already valid continuity bridge authorized by Viktor."]},{"r":"9","cells":["3","Secure incumbent recovery package and commercial protections for delivery, defects, lead time and expedite dependence.","Tadeo Gasparyan","45594","Open - supplier response","Signed corrective action, recovery milestones, capacity commitment, documented freight responsibility and performance remedies; addresses 84.7% on-time, 2.8% defects, 27-day lead time and 11 expedites in July-September.","Do not release the call-off. Tadeo escalates to Viktor; any bridge follows Viktor's documented continuity decision."]},{"r":"10","cells":["4","Release controlled incumbent production call-off for 1 November 2024 to 30 April 2025.","Tadeo Gasparyan","45596","Blocked pending MUST gates","Viktor approval, Ainsley capacity confirmation, Tadeo recovery package, and current incumbent assembly release for Ontario, Canada and Victoria, Australia.","Hold release and execute only the documented time-bounded validated continuity path; do not release the alternate."]},{"r":"11","cells":["5","Ontario, Canada: complete alternate part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality review.","Sigrid Karlsson","45646","In progress - no commitment before 1 November","Sigrid's written Ontario-only production release or written no-release decision; approved-supplier status is insufficient.","Alternate remains a continuity contingency; no Ontario production release."]},{"r":"12","cells":["6","Victoria, Australia: complete alternate part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality review.","Sigrid Karlsson","45646","In progress - no commitment before 1 November","Sigrid's written Victoria-only production release or written no-release decision; Ontario evidence does not clear Victoria.","Alternate remains a continuity contingency; no Victoria production release."]},{"r":"13","cells":["7","Resolve alternate peak-capacity gap: stated 1,500/month versus 1,550 peak leaves a 50-unit/month shortfall.","Ainsley Achterberg","45646","Open - contingency capacity work","Documented alternate capacity plan at or above 1,550 assemblies in the peak month; average-demand sufficiency is not enough.","Alternate remains a continuity contingency and is not eligible for production release."]},{"r":"14","cells":["8","Run incumbent recovery KPI review after award.","Tadeo Gasparyan (commercial); Ainsley provides capacity input","29 Nov 2024; monthly through 30 Apr 2025","Scheduled after award","Monthly report: on-time delivery >=95.0%, incoming defects \u003c=1.5%, lead time \u003c=22 calendar days, and no more than one expedite per month.","Any breach: corrective action within 2 business days and reopen sourcing review; repeat breach in two months or capacity failure escalates to Viktor."]},{"r":"15","cells":["9","Document exception decision for any missed MUST gate before the call-off start.","Viktor Stavropoulos","45596","Standby - only if a gate is missed","Written exception identifies blocked gate, accountable owner, validated bridge supplier/assembly, expiry date, and closure evidence.","No documented exception: retain hold; no unsupported award and no alternate production release."]}],"truncated":false}],"note":"值与公式提取，不还原原始排版；每表最多120行、30列。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/supplier_award_decision_memorandum.docx","bytes":42351,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/1de50528a657c2ef7fc5be8b45519e7e1e9f895936a5d6f1d7da5699624799f0.docx","sha256":"1de50528a657c2ef7fc5be8b45519e7e1e9f895936a5d6f1d7da5699624799f0","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Supplier Award Decision Memorandum\nControl-panel assembly line | Decision record for Halberdine Agricultural\nTo\nJasmin Ricciardi, Halberdine Agricultural\nInternal approver\nViktor Stavropoulos\nAs of\n25 October 2024\nAward period\n1 November 2024 to 30 April 2025\n\nAUTHORIZED DIRECTION  Renew the current approved incumbent for the six-month production call-off only, subject to the MUST gates below. The approved alternate remains a continuity contingency and is not authorized for target-assembly production.\nDecision basis\nThe renewal is the single production path because the incumbent's existing assembly is currently released for both Ontario, Canada and Victoria, Australia, and its stated 1,600/month capacity gives 50 units/month headroom over the 1,550-unit peak. This continuity advantage does not make the incumbent's latest-quarter deterioration acceptable; its recovery package is a release condition. The alternate has a lower quantified commercial case, but its evidence is from a different assembly family, its stated capacity is 50 units/month short of peak, and neither destination has completed part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality release.\nForward commercial view\nSupplier\nBase purchase\nOne-time supplied figures\nProjected expedites\nEvaluated forward cost\nCurrent approved incumbent\n8,400 x $118 = $991,200\nNone stated\n$33,000\n$1,024,200\nApproved alternate\n8,400 x $111 = $932,400\n$24,000 tooling + $9,000 initial premium freight\n$9,600\n$975,000\nForecast: 1,400/month x 6 = 8,400 assemblies; peak month 1,550. Method: each supplier's July-September expedite spend is repeated once for each of the two call-off quarters. The alternate is $49,200 lower in this quantified forward case. The lowest quoted unit price must not be treated as the lowest total-cost decision.\nScope limit: quoted prices, supplied one-time costs, historic expedites and stated capacities are the available figures. This view does not assign values to unvalidated scrap, rework, duty, downtime, line-stop loss or jurisdictional compliance exposure.\nPerformance change: April-June 2024 to July-September 2024\nSupplier\nOn-time delivery\nIncoming defects\nLead time (calendar days)\nExpedites / spend\nCurrent approved incumbent\n96.4% -> 84.7% (-11.7 pp)\n1.1% -> 2.8% (+1.7 pp)\n18 -> 27 days (+9)\n2 / $2,400 -> 11 / $16,500 (+9; +$14,100)\nApproved alternate*\n94.8% -> 93.9% (-0.9 pp)\n1.4% -> 1.6% (+0.2 pp)\n21 -> 22 days (+1)\n3 / $3,600 -> 4 / $4,800 (+1; +$1,200)\nWhy this matters: the incumbent worsened across delivery, defects, lead time and expedite dependence, creating a concrete continuity and containment risk. The alternate is comparatively stable, but *its results are from a different assembly family and are not proof for this target assembly.\n\n\nMUST controls and SHOULD considerations\nBoundary\nApplication to this call-off\nMUST - release, authority and evidence\nBefore any production release: Viktor's written approval because reasonably expected purchasing plus projected expedites is $1,024,200, above the $1,000,000 threshold; Ainsley's written incumbent capacity confirmation; Tadeo's documented recovery and commercial protections; and current release evidence. Sigrid's written part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality release is mandatory before any alternate production use.\nSHOULD - commercial decision considerations\nPrefer the lower defensible evaluated cost only after MUST controls pass; use comparative delivery, defect, lead-time, expedite and continuity evidence; maintain the alternate as a validated continuity option. These are important decision considerations, not substitutes for release or authority.\nDestination and capacity status\nDestination\nCurrent approved incumbent\nApproved alternate\nOntario, Canada\nExisting assembly currently released. Stated 1,600/month vs 1,550 peak = 50-unit/month headroom; confirmation still required.\nApproved-supplier status only. No completed part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality release. Stated 1,500/month vs peak = 50-unit/month shortfall.\nVictoria, Australia\nExisting assembly currently released. Stated 1,600/month vs 1,550 peak = 50-unit/month headroom; confirmation still required.\nApproved-supplier status only. No completed part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality release. Stated 1,500/month vs peak = 50-unit/month shortfall.\nOntario and Victoria remain separate release decisions: evidence or release for one destination does not clear the other. Sigrid estimates six to eight weeks for the alternate reviews, with no commitment that either will close before 1 November 2024.\nAuthority boundary, execution gates and exception path\nPurchasing's supplier-path decision does not constitute engineering, quality or jurisdictional technical approval. Sigrid Karlsson retains engineering and quality release authority. Required sequence: Viktor written approval by 25 October; Ainsley Achterberg's capacity confirmation by 28 October; Tadeo's signed recovery package by 29 October; and Tadeo's release check on 31 October before the 1 November start.\nIF A MUST GATE IS MISSED  Hold the affected production release, escalate the missing gate to its accountable owner, and use only a time-bounded validated continuity bridge with a supplier and assembly already valid for production and Viktor's documented authority. The alternate remains unreleased until each destination has its own written release and its 50-unit peak-capacity gap is closed.\nPost-award control\nTadeo Gasparyan leads the 29 November review and monthly reviews through 30 April 2025 with Ainsley's capacity input. Trigger corrective action and reopen the sourcing review for on-time delivery below 95.0%, incoming defects above 1.5%, lead time above 22 calendar days, or more than one expedite in a month; repeat breach in two months or capacity failure escalates to Viktor.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submission contains a readable, valid DOCX named exactly `supplier_award_decision_memorandum.docx` at the top level.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted DOCX file and its package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, top-level placement, DOCX MIME structure, and that the document opens without repair."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submission contains a readable, valid XLSX named exactly `supplier_award_action_register.xlsx` at the top level.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted XLSX file and its package structure.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact filename, top-level placement, XLSX MIME structure, and that the workbook opens without repair."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The decision memorandum renders to no more than two pages without clipped, hidden, or unreadably compressed decision content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered memorandum.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX with a standard office engine, count pages, and inspect the page edges, tables, and font density."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The action register has exactly one visible worksheet and no more than 12 substantive action rows.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Workbook sheet collection and populated action range.","grader_hint":"Ignore a header row when counting actions; do not count blank formatted rows as substantive."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum makes and records exactly one call-off direction rather than presenting two co-equal options or deferring the decision to a later meeting.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision statement, executive summary, and conclusion.","grader_hint":"A conditional recommendation can qualify if its direction is unambiguous and its conditions are award controls, not an unresolved choice between suppliers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The selected direction is a controlled six-month renewal with the incumbent, subject to required authority and supplier-recovery controls, while the alternate remains a contingency rather than receiving the production call-off before validation.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and award-control language.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the incumbent is the single production call-off choice and the alternate is not described as already releasable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation resolves the commercial tension by explaining why current destination release and adequate peak capacity outweigh the alternate's lower evaluated commercial case for this award, without portraying the incumbent's deteriorating performance as acceptable.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale and risk discussion.","grader_hint":"Look for a balanced rationale that treats release and continuity as gates while requiring recovery from the incumbent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum explicitly states that the lowest quoted unit price must not be treated as the lowest total-cost decision, using equivalent unmistakable wording.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Total-cost warning in the memorandum.","grader_hint":"A generic statement that price is only one factor is insufficient unless it distinguishes quoted unit price from total cost."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum states that the purchasing decision does not constitute engineering, quality, or jurisdictional technical approval.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope or authority boundary in the memorandum.","grader_hint":"Accept concise language reserving technical acceptance to the designated engineering and quality owner."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies an as-of or decision date consistent with 25 October 2024 and the call-off term of 1 November 2024 through 30 April 2025, with date tolerance of ±0 days.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Memo date and award-period statement.","grader_hint":"All three dates must be explicit and calendar-valid."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The six-month forecast quantity equals six times the stated monthly average: 1,400 × 6 = 8,400 assemblies, within ±1 assembly.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Forecast quantity and supporting calculation.","grader_hint":"Credit a displayed formula or a clearly stated 8,400-unit quantity tied to the six-month term."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The commercial comparison uses incumbent and alternate quoted unit prices of $118 and $111 respectively, each within ±$0.01.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cost comparison in the memorandum.","grader_hint":"Confirm supplier labels are not reversed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent's base six-month purchase cost equals forecast quantity multiplied by its quote, 8,400 × $118, within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incumbent cost calculation.","grader_hint":"The resulting base cost should be $991,200 within tolerance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate's base six-month purchase cost equals forecast quantity multiplied by its quote, 8,400 × $111, within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternate cost calculation.","grader_hint":"The resulting base cost should be $932,400 within tolerance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any forward expedite allowance applies one declared method consistently to both suppliers; using the latest-quarter run rate produces $33,000 for the incumbent and $9,600 for the alternate over six months, each within ±$1.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Forward-cost assumptions and calculations.","grader_hint":"Credit another defensible, consistently applied method only if it is explicit and does not selectively favor one supplier; verify its arithmetic."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Under the latest-quarter expedite run-rate assumption, the incumbent's evaluated forward cost equals base purchasing cost plus projected expedites, within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incumbent total evaluated cost.","grader_hint":"The result should be $1,024,200 if the stated run-rate method is used."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate's commercial case includes both the $24,000 transfer-tooling cost and $9,000 initial premium-freight cost, each within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternate cost build-up.","grader_hint":"Confirm neither one-time cost is omitted or treated as a recurring per-unit amount."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Under the latest-quarter expedite run-rate assumption, the alternate's evaluated forward cost equals base cost, transfer tooling, initial premium freight, and projected expedites, within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternate total evaluated cost.","grader_hint":"The result should be $975,000 if the stated run-rate method is used."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The displayed evaluated-cost difference equals the two displayed supplier totals and identifies the alternate as $49,200 lower under the latest-quarter run-rate case, within ±$1.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cost delta and supplier direction.","grader_hint":"Recalculate from the document's totals and confirm that the sign and supplier label are correct."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The cost view clearly separates validated figures from assumptions and open exposure, and does not assign invented values to scrap, rework, duty, downtime, line-stop loss, or jurisdictional compliance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions, exclusions, and total-cost qualification.","grader_hint":"The memorandum may identify these exposures qualitatively but must not present unsupported amounts as facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent on-time comparison shows 96.4% to 84.7%, a deterioration of 11.7 percentage points, within ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"Require percentage-point treatment, not an unlabeled percent-change calculation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent defect comparison shows 1.1% to 2.8%, a deterioration of 1.7 percentage points, within ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"Check both endpoints and the direction of change."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent lead-time comparison shows 18 to 27 calendar days, an increase of 9 days, within ±1 day.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"The document should interpret the increase as adverse for continuity."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent expedite-count comparison shows two to 11 expedites, an increase of nine, within ±0 expedites.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"Confirm the periods and supplier are labeled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent expedite-spend comparison shows $2,400 to $16,500, an increase of $14,100, within ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"Check that spend is not confused with the six-month projection."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate on-time comparison shows 94.8% to 93.9%, a deterioration of 0.9 percentage point, within ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"Require the correct supplier and period labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate defect comparison shows 1.4% to 1.6%, a deterioration of 0.2 percentage point, within ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"Check both endpoints and the direction of change."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate lead-time comparison shows 21 to 22 calendar days, an increase of one day, within ±1 day.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and delta.","grader_hint":"The comparison must not imply this is proven performance for the target assembly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate expedite comparison shows three to four expedites and $3,600 to $4,800, changes of one expedite and $1,200 respectively, with tolerances of ±0 expedites and ±$1.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and deltas.","grader_hint":"Both count and cost must be present and correctly associated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The analysis recognizes the incumbent's broad, material deterioration and the alternate's comparatively stable results while qualifying that the alternate evidence comes from a different assembly family.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Narrative interpretation of the performance table.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the submission draws the useful comparison without treating the datasets as fully equivalent proof."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent's stated capacity of 1,600 per month is compared with the 1,550 peak and correctly shown as 50 units per month of peak headroom.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Capacity comparison.","grader_hint":"Allow equivalent language such as 3.2% headroom if the underlying 50-unit difference is correct within ±1 unit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate's stated capacity of 1,500 per month is compared with the 1,550 peak and correctly shown as a 50-unit peak shortfall requiring resolution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Capacity comparison and treatment in the decision.","grader_hint":"The average demand being below capacity must not be used to hide the peak shortfall."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The decision distinguishes approved-supplier status from part-, line-, and destination-specific production release.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Award-control or MUST/SHOULD section.","grader_hint":"The alternate's appearance on the approved list must not be treated as sufficient authority for production use."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent is shown as currently released for the existing assembly in both Ontario and Victoria, supporting continuity but not excusing its performance decline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction-status comparison and rationale.","grader_hint":"Both destinations must be named."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The alternate is shown as lacking completed part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality release for both Ontario and Victoria.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction-status comparison and award gates.","grader_hint":"Do not credit language that merely says validation is generally in progress without addressing both destinations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Ontario, Canada and Victoria, Australia are assessed separately, with no inference that evidence or release for one destination automatically clears the other.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction comparison.","grader_hint":"The document need not invent technical standards; it must preserve separate review and evidence status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The incumbent forward evaluated cost is tested against the $1,000,000 approval threshold and correctly triggers written approval before release.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Authority analysis and approval action.","grader_hint":"The comparison should use reasonably expected purchasing and expedite cost, not unit-price spend alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum visibly distinguishes MUST controls from SHOULD considerations and places release, authority, and evidence requirements on the mandatory side while treating price preference and comparative performance as decision considerations.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Explicit MUST/SHOULD boundary.","grader_hint":"Look for the literal labels MUST and SHOULD or unmistakably equivalent mandatory/advisory labels applied consistently."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum avoids unsupported claims that either supplier meets particular electrical, labeling, safety, or jurisdictional technical standards.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Technical and jurisdictional statements throughout the memorandum.","grader_hint":"Status statements about release or pending review are acceptable; substantive technical certification claims require evidence that is not available."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A practical exception path states what happens if approval, release, capacity confirmation, or required evidence is not obtained on time: the affected production release is held, escalated to the appropriate owner, and managed through a time-bounded validated continuity path rather than an unsupported award.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contingency language in the memorandum and fallback entries in the register.","grader_hint":"Credit a controlled bridge or no-release escalation; do not credit a vague instruction to review later."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The incumbent renewal is paired with a documented supplier recovery requirement addressing delivery deterioration, defect escalation, lead-time recovery, and expedite dependence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conditions of renewal and action register.","grader_hint":"A generic request to improve performance is insufficient; the recovery work must cover the observed failure modes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Commercial follow-up seeks enforceable or documentable protections appropriate to the observed risk, such as corrective-action commitments, capacity confirmation, recovery milestones, freight responsibility, or performance remedies.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commercial actions and required evidence.","grader_hint":"Do not require a particular contract clause, but look for more than an informal supplier conversation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The alternate's validation and capacity work continues as a structured continuity option without being described as already awarded or technically accepted.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternate-related actions and decision language.","grader_hint":"The work should cover both destination releases and the 50-unit peak-capacity gap."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The work defines measurable post-award review triggers using relevant delivery, defect, lead-time, expedite, capacity, or release evidence and identifies when the sourcing decision will be reopened or escalated.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Monitoring and decision-trigger actions.","grader_hint":"Thresholds may be professionally selected; they must be measurable and connected to a consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Register ownership follows the stated authorities: commercial supplier actions to Tadeo Gasparyan, engineering and quality release to Sigrid Karlsson, production planning and capacity confirmation to Ainsley Achterberg, and over-threshold written approval to Viktor Stavropoulos.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Owner field in the action register.","grader_hint":"Shared ownership is acceptable only when the accountable named owner remains clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Action dates are calendar-valid and logically sequenced so mandatory approval, capacity confirmation, and award conditions precede call-off release, while monitoring follows award.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Due dates and action dependencies in the register.","grader_hint":"Use 25 October and 1 November as key boundaries; flag any plan that releases the call-off before its prerequisites."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The register is executable without asking the author a question: each substantive action identifies an owner, due date, status, required evidence or decision gate, and a specific fallback.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Completed action-register entries.","grader_hint":"Blank or generic entries such as TBD, monitor, or escalate without a recipient or consequence do not satisfy executability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The supplier recommendation and the alternate's contingency status agree between the memorandum and the action register.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision statement in the DOCX and award-related rows in the XLSX.","grader_hint":"Fail this item if the register schedules an alternate production award while the memorandum selects the incumbent, or vice versa."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every volume, unit price, evaluated cost, cost difference, capacity, threshold, and award-period figure repeated in both deliverables is identical within ±1 unit, ±$1, ±0.1 percentage point, or ±0 days as applicable.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"All shared numeric content in the DOCX and XLSX.","grader_hint":"Compare only figures that appear in both files, but audit all such repetitions rather than sampling one."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Shared owners, due dates, approval gates, jurisdiction statuses, and fallback conditions agree between the memorandum and action register.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Narrative commitments in the DOCX and corresponding XLSX entries.","grader_hint":"Minor wording differences are acceptable; responsibility, timing, status, and consequence must not conflict."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Taken together, the two files are concise, executive-ready, externally appropriate, and sufficiently clear for the named customer and internal owners to understand the decision, its evidence, its limits, and what must happen next without follow-up clarification.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall rendered memorandum and action register.","grader_hint":"Consider hierarchy, legibility, tone, decision prominence, terminology consistency, and whether dense detail obscures the call."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A manufacturing buyer must document a high-stakes six-month supplier award for an external agricultural-equipment customer. The commercial evidence favors the alternate in some respects, but capacity, declining incumbent performance, authority limits, and unresolved destination-specific validation create competing controls and risks.","difficulty_sources":["Converting two periods of mixed supplier-performance evidence into material, decision-relevant changes","Applying a consistent forward-cost assumption without inventing unavailable operational costs","Separating mandatory award gates from preferred commercial considerations","Protecting engineering and quality authority while still making one executable purchasing decision","Designing a controlled path for missing approvals, evidence, validation, or capacity confirmation","Maintaining exact agreement between an executive memorandum and an action register"],"authoring_plan":{"solver_instance":"A competent manufacturing purchasing agent preparing an external-facing award record and an internal execution register from a compact set of frozen commercial and operational facts.","behavior_specification":["Transform the two-period evidence into decision-relevant deltas rather than merely restating source values.","Build a forward commercial comparison using a disclosed, consistently applied expedite assumption.","Separate mandatory production-release and authority controls from advisory commercial preferences.","Maintain the boundary between purchasing authority and engineering or quality acceptance.","Record a single award direction, conditions, monitoring triggers, and a failure path in two mutually consistent artifacts."],"planted_imperfections_and_tensions":["The incumbent is currently usable and has sufficient peak capacity, but every supplied performance indicator deteriorates materially in the latest period.","The alternate has the lower quote and lower quantified forward commercial case, but its evidence comes from another assembly family.","The alternate's stated monthly capacity covers average demand but misses peak demand.","Destination-specific technical reviews remain unresolved and cannot be converted into purchasing conclusions.","The selected incumbent case crosses the written-approval threshold only when reasonably expected expedite cost is included.","Several consequential cost categories are unknown, preventing a false-precision total-cost claim.","The normal award process may fail to complete by the release date, requiring a controlled hold, escalation, or validated bridge."],"gold_relationships":{"period_changes":["For each service measure, compute later period minus earlier period and preserve the correct unit: percentage points, days, counts, or currency.","Interpret the incumbent changes as a broad deterioration and the alternate changes as comparatively stable, while retaining the comparability caveat."],"forecast_and_capacity":["Six-month quantity equals monthly average multiplied by six.","Peak headroom or shortfall equals stated monthly capacity minus peak-month demand.","Average-capacity sufficiency does not cure a peak-capacity shortfall."],"commercial_case":["Base purchase cost equals forecast quantity multiplied by quoted unit price.","A defensible forward comparison applies the same declared treatment of recent expedite experience to both suppliers.","Using the latest-quarter run rate, multiply each latest quarterly expedite amount by two for the six-month horizon.","Add transfer tooling and initial premium freight only to the alternate case.","The evaluated-cost difference equals the two calculated supplier totals and must use the correct sign.","Unknown scrap, rework, duty, downtime, line-stop, and technical-compliance exposure remains qualitative."],"authority_and_release":["Compare reasonably expected purchasing plus expedite cost with the stated approval threshold rather than comparing unit-price spend alone.","Approved-supplier status is not equivalent to part-, line-, or destination-specific production release.","Each named destination retains a separate release status.","Purchasing may decide and document the supplier path but may not make technical acceptance findings."],"cross_artifact_consistency":["Any recommendation, supplier status, figure, owner, date, gate, or fallback repeated in both deliverables must match.","Action timing must place mandatory evidence and approvals before production release."]},"intended_verdict":"Record a controlled six-month incumbent renewal because it is the only currently released option with stated peak capacity, despite its higher evaluated cost and deteriorating performance. Make written over-threshold approval, capacity confirmation, and a documented supplier recovery package prerequisites to release. Continue destination-specific validation and peak-capacity resolution for the alternate as a contingency, not as a completed production award.","must_should_boundary":{"must":["Written authority for an over-threshold commitment before release.","Part-, line-, and destination-specific engineering and quality release before production use.","Evidence sufficient to support peak capacity and award conditions.","A hold and escalation path when a required gate cannot be completed."],"should":["Prefer the lower defensible evaluated cost when mandatory controls are satisfied.","Use comparative delivery, quality, lead-time, expedite, and continuity evidence to shape the commercial decision.","Maintain a validated alternate and measurable supplier-performance triggers."]},"failure_path":["Do not convert missing approval, release, capacity evidence, or technical evidence into an assumption of completion.","Hold the affected production release and route the unresolved gate to its accountable owner.","Use only a time-bounded continuity arrangement involving a supplier and assembly already valid for production, with documented authority.","Define the evidence and date that either closes the exception or reopens the sourcing decision."],"vocabulary_and_local_conventions":["The solver is expected to distinguish MUST controls from SHOULD considerations; the request supplies those labels.","The action register must use the request-specified concepts of owner, due date, status, required evidence or decision gate, and fallback.","No private supplier codes, technical certification codes, or unstated policy thresholds are required."],"offline_validation":"An imagined reference solution was arithmetically checked using the stated quantities, quotes, one-time costs, latest-quarter expedite run rates, capacities, dates, and approval threshold. The memo and register can be produced with standard office libraries and rendered offline within the stated space limits, and every rubric item is reachable without internet access or unstated local codes."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"supplier_award_memo","filename":"supplier_award_decision_memorandum.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"External-facing executive supplier award decision memorandum comparing the two performance periods and recording one call-off direction.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be no more than two rendered pages.","Must make exactly one supplier-path recommendation.","Must be suitable for the named external customer and procurement director.","Must not invent unvalidated costs or make technical acceptance claims on engineering's behalf."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"supplier_award_register","filename":"supplier_award_action_register.xlsx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet","description":"Executable register of award actions, owners, timing, gates, evidence, status, and fallback actions supporting the memorandum.","required":true,"constraints":["Must contain one worksheet.","Must contain no more than 12 action rows.","Must include action, owner, due date, status, required evidence or decision gate, and fallback information.","All shared recommendations, dates, costs, volumes, thresholds, and other figures must agree with the memorandum."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"buyers_purchasing_agents","sector":"manufacturing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be reviewed independently by one senior manufacturing procurement practitioner and one reviewer experienced in supplier quality or engineering-release governance. Reviewers must verify arithmetic, authority boundaries, jurisdiction-status wording, executability, and cross-file consistency.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No supplier quotations, purchasing-policy document, technical standards, or validation records are supplied beyond the frozen facts in the request.","The reference decision therefore must not assert substantive technical compliance or monetize unvalidated operational exposure.","Professionally selected monitoring thresholds may vary if they are measurable, proportionate, and linked to an explicit consequence."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:48:17+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:48:17+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:48:17+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:48:17+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/0de0c9bfc707c2c7","declared_deliverables":["supplier_award_decision_memorandum.docx","supplier_award_action_register.xlsx"]};
